/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  mhv01_methanol_water_100C --- CAN A CUBIC BE FED A LOW-PRESSURE G^E MODEL?

  Twelve bubble points of methanol (1) + water (2) at 100 C, the MEASURED
  vapour-liquid equilibrium of Griswold & Wong (1952) as printed in Poling,
  Prausnitz & O'Connell, 5th ed., Table 8-46 ("Experimental"), p. 8.146:
  760.0 to 2650.9 mmHg, x1 = 0 to 1.  Each state is asked six ways, by name (mhv1, mhv2 and oneFluid on the fitted
  Mathias-Copeman alpha, mhv1Soave and mhv2Soave on the plain Soave alpha):

    gammaPhi   (the system)  the catalogue's NRTL pair, ideal-gas vapour, the
                             records' own vapour pressures: the frame the pair
                             was REGRESSED in.
    mhv1                     SRK with the Modified Huron-Vidal first-order
                             mixing rule (Michelsen 1990) fed the SAME pair.
    mhv2                     SRK with the second-order rule (Dahl & Michelsen
                             1990), the SAME pair.
    oneFluid                 SRK with the van der Waals one-fluid rule, kij = 0
                             (no G^E at all: what the cubic does alone).

  Each op reports P_bubble (Pa) and y_eqT_methanol (the incipient vapour).  The
  measurements are in the golden file as `anchor` rows with the band stated in
  the README.  THE PAIR IS NOT THE RULE'S: the catalogue pair was fitted at
  297-361 K and 15-95 kPa and this is 373 K and up to 3.5 bar, so what the
  numbers measure is the rule's use of the pair AND the pair's own error --
  see the README for which is which.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
